e Vertebrate Endocrinology</source><publisher-loc>Cambridge</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Cambridge University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005111-Nelson1"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nelson</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><source>An Introduction to Behavioral Endocrinology</source><publisher-loc>Sunderland, MA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Sinauer Associates, Inc</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005111-Axelrod1"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Axelrod</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Reisine</surname><given-names>TD</given-names></name></person-group><year>1984</year><article-title>Stress Hormones - Their Interaction and Regulation.</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>224</volume><fpage>452</fpage><lpage>459</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6143403</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005111-Creel1"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Creel</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Social dominance and stress hormones.</article-title><source>Trends Ecol Evol</source><volume>16</volume><fpage>491</fpage><lpage>497</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0005111-Moore1"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>IT</given-names></name><name><surname>Jessop</surname><given-names>TS</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Stress, reproduction, and adrenocortical modulation in amphibians and reptiles.</article-title><source>Horm Behav</source><volume>43</volume><fpage>39</fpage><lpage>47</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12614633</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="